The attached image conceptually illustrates the dendrimer-like structure of the alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles (Nano-11) discussed in the paper. It depicts a spherical particle with a dense, highly branched internal network radiating from a central core. This structure is characteristic of phytoglycogen nanoparticles, contributing to their high molecular density and irregular, cauliflower-like appearance observed via TEM (Fig. 2). This dendrimer-like architecture is key to Nano-11's properties, including its high adsorptive capacity for negatively charged protein antigens like ovalbumin.